WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:02.800
Good evening, everyone.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:03.300
Hi.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.790
My name is Larry
Gallagher, and on behalf

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:09.600
of MIT Video Productions and
MIT Music and Theater Arts,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.480
I'd like to welcome you to
this very special program.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.570
I'd particularly like to welcome
MIT's President and First

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.730
Lady, Rafael and Chris Reif.

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:24.520
And of course our very
special guest, Jacob Collier.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:30.020
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:32.479
Jacob's residency
last fall at MIT

00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:34.340
is the subject of
the documentary

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.770
that we're about to screen.

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:38.450
Following the
screening, Fred Harris,

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:41.600
the Director of MIT's
wind and jazz ensemble

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:46.760
will moderate a
brief Q&amp;A session

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.940
that is going to
include key contributors

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.390
to the film and the concert.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:56.900
The evening's
program will conclude

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:01.090
with a special
performance by Jacob.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:04.769
And so Jean Dunoyer,
the film's director,

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:07.350
will introduce the
film in just a moment.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:10.530
But first, I'm very pleased
to introduce MIT's President,

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:11.430
Rafael Reif.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.430
Well good evening, everyone.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:21.580
There was no way I was
going to miss this evening.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:22.570
No way.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:24.410
Three reasons.

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:26.800
One is, never in
my life I've been

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.520
invited to a movie premiere.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.950
This is my shot and I was
not going to miss that.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:34.490
That's number one.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:39.250
Number two, I really wanted
to meet Jacob Collier.

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:40.530
Very much wanted to meet him.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:42.140
I've heard so much
about him, and I

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.950
wanted to hear about
himself, and about his music,

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.340
and about what he did at MIT.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.760
And I also heard
that he has claimed

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.280
that MIT is his second home,
so I will come here to say,

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.660
welcome back home, Jacob.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:58.622
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:58.622 --> 00:01:59.830
And there was a third reason.

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:02.510
There is one other reason
why I wanted to be here,

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:09.190
and that is to recognize one of
our most special talent at MIT.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:12.400
This is a fellow who is
known in some circles

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.610
as the Ken Burns of MIT.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.410
Do you know who
I'm referring to?

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:20.250
I'm talking about
Mr. Larry Gallagher.

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.470
I think all of you know,
all of you, I'm sure,

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:32.690
know, that Larry
and his MVP team,

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:35.290
they got an Emmy
award over the summer

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:38.140
for his documentary,
A Bold Move, that

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:42.820
describes the move of MIT
from Back Bay to Cambridge.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.960
And that was not it.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.830
That was his second Emmy award,
because the same team got

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:52.780
an Emmy award a couple of years
ago, both of them very richly

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:53.280
deserved.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.330
So I want to take this
opportunity, Larry,

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:59.410
to say to you, thank you for
telling the MIT story with so

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:01.980
much care, love, and elegance.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:05.440
And to your team, the MVP
team, for all the terrific work

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.000
they do for MIT.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:08.860
Thank you so very much,
and congratulations.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:23.390
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:24.500
Well, can you hear me?

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:25.860
It's a thrill to be here.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.450
It's a thrill to be sharing
this film with you tonight.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:34.280
And thank you very much, Rafael,
for those very kind words.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.150
This is really a
passion project,

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:38.450
so it was extraordinary
to spend all this time

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:40.670
with so many people,
many of which

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:44.570
are in this room tonight, who
have so much energy to create.

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:48.200
And I think that that energy,
for creation and doing it

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.380
in a collaborative way,
is kind of a way for us

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:55.520
to enjoy our lives
to the fullest.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:58.040
And so I think this project
was definitely that for me,

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:00.140
and I hope for all the
others who are here

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:02.600
tonight in the audience.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:07.040
And also, to make
documentary films,

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.190
the hardest part
is funding them.

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:14.150
And so I wanted to give my
gratitude to Neil and Jane

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:18.470
Pappalardo who have given MIT
Video Productions the space

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:21.709
to create, to tell the
incredible stories that

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:24.890
are going on every day at MIT.

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:28.086
So we get a chance to
pick those stories,

00:04:28.086 --> 00:04:29.710
and tonight you get
to see one of them.

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:31.280
So I hope you like it.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.859
And I'll be here afterwards
if you have any questions

00:04:33.859 --> 00:04:35.650
or you want to hear me
talk about the film.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:38.110
I could go on forever.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:38.690
All right?

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:40.070
Enjoy the show.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:41.370
And it's about to start.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:49.080
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:50.540
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:52.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:59.820
We are making music with one
of the most talented people

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:02.190
I've ever met named
Jacob Collier.

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:05.430
So you go, (SINGING) you
don't wanna be my savior.

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:09.120
And you don't want to--
especially with the--

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.100
you don't wanna be my savior.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.520
In the music world,
Jacob's huge, you know?

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:15.210
And so in some
ways, for him to get

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:17.502
to work with all these
musicians, it's the best thing.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.090
We were able to get
him to come to MIT

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:28.766
and kind of do a
week long residency.

00:05:33.248 --> 00:05:35.660
MIT's visiting artist
program is trying

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:39.950
to bring great artists
in substantive contact

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:41.390
with our students.

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:43.820
It's all about music
education experiences.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:47.790
I wanted them to feel the
sparks of Jacob's creativity

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:51.950
firsthand, and I wanted
them to go beyond that,

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:55.560
to get inside his music and to
be able to make it their own.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:58.000
For this kind of thing, it's--
all you really need is--

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:04.765
is like a-- you
know what I mean?

00:06:04.765 --> 00:06:09.635
More than like-- you know,
you could even find like a--

00:06:09.635 --> 00:06:11.260
I mean, like do your
own thing, but you

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.385
could find a riffy thing
that's just kind of like--

00:06:15.786 --> 00:06:16.660
you know what I mean?

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:18.950
And just like-- because
I guess the guitar is

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:20.731
like basically a
drummer here as opposed

00:06:20.731 --> 00:06:21.980
to being a harmony instrument.

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:23.743
He creates a really
great atmosphere

00:06:23.743 --> 00:06:26.159
for working with people and
bringing the best out of them.

00:06:36.687 --> 00:06:37.520
That sounds amazing.

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:43.200
It's a creative environment,
and that really feeds me.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.300
Their minds are obviously
so sharp, you know?

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.550
And it's not even
necessarily musically sharp

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:50.802
in the foreground, but these
guys think about stuff.

00:06:50.802 --> 00:06:53.260
There's this gig on Saturday
that if don't have tickets to,

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:55.510
I believe there's a waiting
list, but you should come.

00:06:55.510 --> 00:06:57.180
It's going to be so crazy--

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:58.380
200 musicians on the stage.

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:06.070
I'm performing with Jacob
Collier, which is amazing.

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:17.350
When I listen to his music,
it like sends chills up

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:18.860
my spine, especially "Hideaway."

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:20.109
Like, that's my favorite song.

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:29.276
A sense of peace
a sense of calm,

00:07:29.276 --> 00:07:30.400
and something making sense.

00:07:42.887 --> 00:07:44.470
When I started
listening to the music,

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:47.500
I realized this was a
lot more complicated

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:49.795
than most arranging projects.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:03.500
As a music nerd, it's a
beautiful looking score to me

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:05.164
because there's all
these things that

00:08:05.164 --> 00:08:07.580
didn't appear like they should
be there from the beginning

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:09.492
and they start to show up.

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:26.200
His music is in many ways
like classical music, which

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:30.160
is that so much of
it is specified.

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:41.050
Take me anywhere you want to go.

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:51.030
You know that my love is strong.

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:55.590
It's pretty moving for some--
well, "Hideaway" especially.

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:57.180
There are certain
points in the music

00:08:57.180 --> 00:08:59.230
where it's pretty overwhelming.

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:00.730
[MUSIC - JACOB COLLIER,
 "HIDEAWAY"]

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:09.869
(SINGING) Softly, like the
calm that follows storms

00:09:09.869 --> 00:09:11.620
So he states the melody.

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:12.950
He sings the melody.

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:16.220
It's all very tonal and
pretty like a pop song.

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:20.600
And then we get to the
bridge and suddenly he

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:22.100
throws us for a loop.

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:23.416
(SINGING) In my hideaway.

00:09:30.388 --> 00:09:44.332
Even when I close
my eyes, darling I

00:09:44.332 --> 00:10:00.268
will always stay wide
awake in my hideaway.

00:10:06.742 --> 00:10:19.900
Touch me like I've never
loved before in the place

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:29.969
the I adore in my hideaway.

00:10:29.969 --> 00:10:32.010
The drummer has one of
the hardest things of all.

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:34.500
He's got to play the
fives on the symbol

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:36.670
but still maintain the
slow four with his foot.

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:47.086
(SINIGING) I know whichever
way the wind may blow,

00:10:47.086 --> 00:10:56.014
there will be a place for
me to go in my hideaway.

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:20.318
Whether you be lost
or found, darling, I

00:11:20.318 --> 00:11:48.320
will always stay wide awake
in my hideaway, my hideaway.

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:50.720
Down, falling down.

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:52.640
Down, falling down.

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:55.040
Down, falling down.

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:56.960
Down, falling down.

00:11:56.960 --> 00:11:59.870
In the second verse, he
adds this layer of fives.

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:01.518
So, 3, 4, 5.

00:12:01.518 --> 00:12:04.138
[TICKING]

00:12:07.487 --> 00:12:09.070
And it's made more
complex by the fact

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:11.785
that the fives are grouped in
fours and threes and sixes.

00:12:14.545 --> 00:12:31.690
Falling down, falling down,
falling down to the ground

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:34.280
It's-- you know, you don't
hear the math of it when

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:35.860
you're listening to the song.

00:12:35.860 --> 00:12:37.740
You just suddenly
become unmoored

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:39.500
in this kind of dreamlike state.

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:42.542
And yet you still
hear the slow pulse

00:12:42.542 --> 00:12:44.000
from the original
part of the song.

00:12:47.276 --> 00:12:51.380
He's kind of playing with this
notion of you, the listener,

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:53.810
thinking you know where you are.

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:57.590
And he'll throw something
at you that unmoors it

00:12:57.590 --> 00:13:00.915
but without completely
divorcing you from the structure

00:13:00.915 --> 00:13:01.790
that you knew before.

00:13:19.718 --> 00:13:23.204
[OOING]

00:13:41.132 --> 00:13:43.620
It's just-- it's a revelation.

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:44.920
It's amazing.

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:49.410
And all of this is happening
in what at the beginning

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:52.650
seemed to be a simple
four chord pop song.

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:56.129
[SCATTING]

00:14:01.954 --> 00:14:03.370
This is kind of
what our lives are

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:06.460
like is that we try to
reduce them so that we

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:07.840
can get through the day.

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:11.950
But there's stuff going on that
if we just pay attention to it

00:14:11.950 --> 00:14:15.370
is beautiful,
inspiring, frightening.

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:19.270
And he's kind of encapsulating
all of this in a five or six

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:22.446
minute song that appears to be
a pop ballad at the beginning.

00:14:22.446 --> 00:14:25.422
[SCATTING]

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:38.318
Let me feel the sky
and feel the moon.

00:14:38.318 --> 00:14:46.750
Let me sing and always
tune to my hideaway.

00:14:50.270 --> 00:14:52.220
It was just like
everything I had ever

00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:57.110
wanted to hear in music because
it's so intricate in detail,

00:14:57.110 --> 00:15:00.530
but it's purposeful, and like,
he has a purpose for everything

00:15:00.530 --> 00:15:04.367
he does, every note he hits,
every weird beat that he plays.

00:15:04.367 --> 00:15:10.828
Find a home in everything
in my hideaway.

00:15:17.786 --> 00:15:28.720
Maybe you can come to stay.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:48.600
And I will meet you here
someday far away in my hideaway.

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:11.430
What my mom did is
recognize that I

00:16:11.430 --> 00:16:14.130
had some kind of weird brain
that was very thirsty and very

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:17.400
inventive and quite
emotionally mature

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:20.100
and presented lots of
different things to me.

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:21.090
Like, this is Bach.

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:21.995
This is Stravinsky.

00:16:21.995 --> 00:16:22.620
This is Bartok.

00:16:22.620 --> 00:16:23.570
This is Britain.
This is Earth, Wind, and Fire.

00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:24.555
This is Bobby McFerrin.

00:16:24.555 --> 00:16:25.150
This is Sting.

00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:28.200
Those are like a lot of
my heroes growing up.

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:30.180
And I wasn't told to
do anything with them.

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:31.430
I was just told to enjoy them.

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:33.638
She used to do things like
turn on the vacuum cleaner

00:16:33.638 --> 00:16:35.750
or, as we say in the UK,
the Hoover and plug it in

00:16:35.750 --> 00:16:37.860
and it would go [VACUUM SOUND]
and she would say,

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:39.190
Jacob, what note
does that feel like?

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:40.710
This is when I'm
two, two years old.

00:16:40.710 --> 00:16:42.280
I was like, G?

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:44.310
yeah, that's a G.
Really, the biggest gift

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:47.194
I was given as a child was
a space and the affirmation

00:16:47.194 --> 00:16:47.860
to create in it.

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:51.700
And I was never, ever
taught to practice ever.

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:53.010
I was never taught to practice.

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:54.260
And people don't believe that.

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:55.490
No, no way.

00:16:55.490 --> 00:16:56.890
Where's the discipline?

00:16:56.890 --> 00:16:59.760
Sort of thing, and it's
funny because it's true,

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:02.270
but people have an
idea of how to learn

00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:06.012
and how to teach that I think
is horrifically out of date.

00:17:06.012 --> 00:17:13.288
Do my dreaming and my scheming.

00:17:13.288 --> 00:17:17.112
Lie awake and pray.

00:17:20.860 --> 00:17:28.135
Do my crying and my sighing.

00:17:28.135 --> 00:17:30.760
Laugh at yesterday.

00:17:36.688 --> 00:17:42.122
Now it's dark and I'm alone.

00:17:42.122 --> 00:18:00.400
But I won't be afraid in my
room, in my room, in my room,

00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:04.109
in my room.

00:18:04.109 --> 00:18:06.150
I like the idea of at the
beginning of "Hajanga,"

00:18:06.150 --> 00:18:07.670
the first thing you hear
is the strings come in.

00:18:07.670 --> 00:18:09.140
It's like the announcing
of a new song.

00:18:09.140 --> 00:18:09.550
Yeah.

00:18:09.550 --> 00:18:10.950
So I quite like the change
of texture for that.

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:11.450
That's cool.

00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:12.860
Do you want to do those edits?

00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:14.860
You can plug in the
speakers and do those edits.

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:16.070
I'd love that, yeah.

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:17.310
Can we plug in a microphone?

00:18:17.310 --> 00:18:17.810
Yeah.

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:18.360
Ah, sweet.

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:21.524
[SINGING]

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:25.340
I saw one of his
videos on YouTube

00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:27.560
and I thought, oh my
gosh, this guy's amazing.

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:30.740
He's got this great sense of
aesthetics and editing skills

00:18:30.740 --> 00:18:32.574
and mixing and all these things.

00:18:32.574 --> 00:18:34.115
And I like to build
music tech stuff.

00:18:34.115 --> 00:18:36.510
And so I sent him a
Facebook message saying--

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:38.080
I'm at MIT and I make stuff.

00:18:38.080 --> 00:18:40.038
Have you any ideas of
stuff you'd like to make?

00:18:40.038 --> 00:18:41.282
And I said, oh, yes I have.

00:18:41.282 --> 00:18:43.532
I've always dreamed of
singing harmony on the spot,

00:18:43.532 --> 00:18:45.240
be able to essentially
improvise a choir.

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:47.450
Singing in the life time.

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:52.000
Take these broken wings
and learn to fly, oh, oh.

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:54.920
A harmonizer takes
whatever you're singing.

00:18:54.920 --> 00:18:57.380
And there's a keyboard and
you can play different notes.

00:18:57.380 --> 00:18:59.810
And the notes that you
play are transformed

00:18:59.810 --> 00:19:03.370
so that what you sing is spread
across lots and lots of notes--

00:19:03.370 --> 00:19:07.310
basically multiplies
his voice up in stacks.

00:19:07.310 --> 00:19:12.950
Take these broken
wings and learn to fly.

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:18.622
Oh, all your life
you were only--

00:19:18.622 --> 00:19:23.096
you were only waiting for this
moment to-- moment to be free.

00:19:23.096 --> 00:19:28.026
Blackbird, fly.

00:19:28.026 --> 00:19:31.477
Blackbird, fly.

00:19:39.860 --> 00:19:41.779
Because this is like
woodwind and then sax.

00:19:41.779 --> 00:19:43.320
Those sounds go
together, don't they?

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:44.020
Yeah.

00:19:44.020 --> 00:19:47.050
I'm going to be creating this
kind of improvised harmonic,

00:19:47.050 --> 00:19:51.570
more spontaneous technological
musical compound piece where

00:19:51.570 --> 00:19:53.650
I can send the
musicians information

00:19:53.650 --> 00:19:56.690
during the performance via
maybe iPad screens or something

00:19:56.690 --> 00:19:58.702
like this where I say,
you play these notes now.

00:19:58.702 --> 00:19:59.410
And they play it.

00:19:59.410 --> 00:20:00.610
It swells up and swells down.

00:20:00.610 --> 00:20:01.943
And then I can fill in the gaps.

00:20:01.943 --> 00:20:04.080
And it's like a
big conversation.

00:20:04.080 --> 00:20:05.830
And this is one of the
main pieces of work

00:20:05.830 --> 00:20:07.840
that will be done
between now and December.

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:11.620
I think on stage,
you'll need one laptop.

00:20:11.620 --> 00:20:13.371
That will be the MIDI input.

00:20:13.371 --> 00:20:13.870
OK.

00:20:13.870 --> 00:20:15.120
And that will have a keyboard.

00:20:27.620 --> 00:20:34.260
We each open up a web page on
our phone, and there's a staff.

00:20:34.260 --> 00:20:37.020
So he's picking all these
different notes, chords,

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:40.200
rhythms, everything for
every different instrument.

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:43.470
And we're playing them as
they come up on our screen.

00:20:43.470 --> 00:20:47.116
It's allowing Jacob to literally
play the band in real time.

00:21:05.470 --> 00:21:06.152
I'm a scientist.

00:21:06.152 --> 00:21:07.860
I'm an engineer too,
so I like to tinker.

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:09.530
I like to explore things.

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:13.380
And one of my best
laboratories is with music.

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:16.010
So I think if anything
Jacob, has made us

00:21:16.010 --> 00:21:20.990
into bolder, more risk
taking experimentalists.

00:21:20.990 --> 00:21:23.540
I learned perhaps more
about how it's best

00:21:23.540 --> 00:21:27.065
not to learn and not to
teach than how to learn

00:21:27.065 --> 00:21:27.930
and how to teach.

00:21:27.930 --> 00:21:30.170
Don't give people all
the answers to things.

00:21:30.170 --> 00:21:31.628
If you give people
all the answers,

00:21:31.628 --> 00:21:33.420
then they are the
subject to your criteria.

00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:35.628
But if you give them all
the questions and the clues,

00:21:35.628 --> 00:21:37.250
then they find
their own answers.

00:21:37.250 --> 00:21:40.820
And actually, the process of
finding an answer is almost all

00:21:40.820 --> 00:21:41.542
of learning.

00:21:41.542 --> 00:21:43.250
So I think teachers
have a responsibility

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:46.584
to trust their students
to find not only answers,

00:21:46.584 --> 00:21:49.250
but their own answers that might
be different from the teacher's

00:21:49.250 --> 00:21:51.166
answers, especially when
it comes to something

00:21:51.166 --> 00:21:54.469
like jazz which is so vague
and so expressive and personal.

00:21:54.469 --> 00:21:57.834
(SINGING) If I let
your mind create me,

00:21:57.834 --> 00:22:01.292
the work will never end.

00:22:01.292 --> 00:22:04.256
I don't wanna be a preacher.

00:22:04.256 --> 00:22:07.220
I just wanna be a man.

00:22:07.220 --> 00:22:10.184
I won't let you exaltate me.

00:22:10.184 --> 00:22:12.654
Just to keep me as I am.

00:22:12.654 --> 00:22:15.618
I don't wanna be a---

00:22:15.618 --> 00:22:18.582
We have to take
the cue from him.

00:22:18.582 --> 00:22:20.150
I had an idea for like--

00:22:20.150 --> 00:22:22.970
for the section that
it goes [VOCALIZING]..

00:22:22.970 --> 00:22:24.750
I could be there like
with the [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:25.680
I'm getting stuff.

00:22:25.680 --> 00:22:28.470
[VOCALIZING]

00:22:29.870 --> 00:22:31.532
Just to give them
the rhythm there.

00:22:31.532 --> 00:22:32.918
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:22:32.918 --> 00:22:34.685
[INAUDIBLE]

00:22:34.685 --> 00:22:35.185
OK.

00:22:39.232 --> 00:22:40.690
At what stage in
the process do you

00:22:40.690 --> 00:22:42.300
write the lyrics to the song?

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:42.800
It depends.

00:22:42.800 --> 00:22:44.360
Sometimes they're the
first thing that I write.

00:22:44.360 --> 00:22:45.080
I write a lot.

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:46.663
I write-- I have a
thought book that's

00:22:46.663 --> 00:22:48.680
filled with words,
really dense imagery,

00:22:48.680 --> 00:22:50.750
like really interesting,
dense imagery.

00:22:50.750 --> 00:22:51.620
And when I come to write lyrics.

00:22:51.620 --> 00:22:53.120
I try and write as
little of it as I

00:22:53.120 --> 00:22:55.340
can because the music
is already complex

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:58.104
and I need to allow
people to find

00:22:58.104 --> 00:22:59.270
their meanings in the words.

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:01.020
Like the song "Hajanga"
it's just a number

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:02.360
of seven syllable lines.

00:23:02.360 --> 00:23:05.330
Everybody near and far,
come together as you are.

00:23:05.330 --> 00:23:08.080
And to the ocean, to the sky,
sing that cosmic lullaby.

00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:09.830
That could mean lots
of different things.

00:23:09.830 --> 00:23:11.045
That could mean life and death.

00:23:11.045 --> 00:23:13.100
It could mean a simple
song that your mother sings

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:13.820
to you before you go to sleep.

00:23:13.820 --> 00:23:15.760
It could mean the
actually universe-- like,

00:23:15.760 --> 00:23:16.801
lots of different things.

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:27.260
I'd rather write words
that invite people

00:23:27.260 --> 00:23:29.740
to understand them rather
than project my understanding

00:23:29.740 --> 00:23:30.490
onto other people.

00:23:55.732 --> 00:24:07.030
Everybody near and far
come together as you are.

00:24:10.945 --> 00:24:22.160
And to the ocean, to the sky,
sing that cosmic lullaby.

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:24.368
Sing that lullaby.

00:24:24.368 --> 00:24:25.844
Sing the hajanga.

00:24:35.684 --> 00:24:44.111
Sing your pleasure,
sing your pain

00:24:44.111 --> 00:24:46.606
like I'll never sing again.

00:24:50.598 --> 00:24:58.670
Let it echo loud
and clear across

00:24:58.670 --> 00:25:01.063
the ancient stratosphere.

00:25:06.244 --> 00:25:13.190
And when the good
days pass you by,

00:25:13.190 --> 00:25:17.404
there's a spark of
joy that can be found.

00:25:20.771 --> 00:25:29.530
And when those dark clouds bring
you down lift your hands up

00:25:29.530 --> 00:25:31.410
from the sky singing

00:25:31.410 --> 00:25:34.386
[SCATTING]

00:26:01.666 --> 00:26:09.230
As the words go round and
round and round and round,

00:26:09.230 --> 00:26:12.950
let the tears roll
down and down.

00:26:12.950 --> 00:26:15.740
[SCATTING]

00:26:16.240 --> 00:26:24.060
Sing the way you
wish to be across

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:26.520
the ancient stratosphere.

00:26:31.932 --> 00:26:38.054
Even when those dark
clouds bring you down,

00:26:38.054 --> 00:26:44.832
there's a spark of
joy that can be found.

00:26:44.832 --> 00:26:50.664
Oh, even when things
break and fall apart.

00:26:53.580 --> 00:26:57.546
Lift your voices from
your heart singing--

00:26:57.546 --> 00:27:00.438
[SCATTING]

00:27:07.668 --> 00:27:10.880
So every morning
and every night,

00:27:10.880 --> 00:27:19.422
from that darkness to the light,
like a beacon shining bright,

00:27:19.422 --> 00:27:20.895
sing the hajanga.

00:27:20.895 --> 00:27:22.130
You'll be all right.

00:27:22.130 --> 00:27:25.918
From the winter
comes the spring.

00:27:25.918 --> 00:27:30.176
It don't matter
what life may bring.

00:27:30.176 --> 00:27:33.669
Said you can do most anything.

00:27:33.669 --> 00:27:37.661
So give your hajanga
a song to sing.

00:27:37.661 --> 00:27:40.655
So tell your mama
and tell your pa.

00:27:40.655 --> 00:27:44.647
Sing it near and sing it far.

00:27:44.647 --> 00:27:49.138
Be exactly the way you are.

00:27:49.138 --> 00:27:50.635
See the hajanga.

00:27:50.635 --> 00:27:52.631
It's your guiding star.

00:27:52.631 --> 00:27:59.118
So every woman, every man, in
every nation, in every land,

00:27:59.118 --> 00:28:03.110
I said please you've
got to understand.

00:28:03.110 --> 00:28:07.102
Now sing the hajanga
and take my hand.

00:28:31.553 --> 00:28:34.632
Then one day, your
life is through.

00:28:38.790 --> 00:28:42.100
Nothing more that you can do.

00:28:45.892 --> 00:28:53.099
So give away the things
you know, things you know.

00:28:53.099 --> 00:29:00.057
And tell your friends
you love them so.

00:29:00.057 --> 00:29:01.548
Tell them hajanga.

00:29:52.084 --> 00:29:54.000
Probably busy this whole
week, but if you ever

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:56.699
find like an hour and you want
to go grab a pint anywhere--

00:29:56.699 --> 00:29:57.615
yeah, yeah, fantastic.

00:29:57.615 --> 00:29:58.950
Are you're going-- you
coming on Saturday?

00:29:58.950 --> 00:29:59.451
I am.

00:29:59.451 --> 00:30:01.449
Just find me after the--
find me after the show.

00:30:01.449 --> 00:30:02.294
I'll be hanging out.

00:30:02.294 --> 00:30:02.736
Cool, all right.

00:30:02.736 --> 00:30:03.235
Fantastic.

00:30:03.235 --> 00:30:03.790
All right.

00:30:03.790 --> 00:30:04.190
So--

00:30:04.190 --> 00:30:05.270
Yeah, yeah, so nice
to meet you too.

00:30:05.270 --> 00:30:05.910
Yeah, see you soon.

00:30:05.910 --> 00:30:06.409
Thank you.

00:30:06.409 --> 00:30:08.630
Cheers, see you later.

00:30:08.630 --> 00:30:13.350
I really feel like MIT is a sort
of second home in some ways.

00:30:13.350 --> 00:30:15.420
I've always felt
some kind of kinship

00:30:15.420 --> 00:30:18.490
with this kind of idea of
celebrating the introvert.

00:30:18.490 --> 00:30:21.930
At MIT, it's a sort of sublime
understanding of the people

00:30:21.930 --> 00:30:23.065
who think inwards first.

00:30:23.065 --> 00:30:25.840
And I would count myself in
amongst that group of people.

00:30:25.840 --> 00:30:29.689
So I'd like to celebrate that
idea, I think, and being at MIT

00:30:29.689 --> 00:30:31.230
consistently reminds
me how wonderful

00:30:31.230 --> 00:30:34.290
it is when people
think beyond this level

00:30:34.290 --> 00:30:36.760
and up and down into the
other realms of things.

00:30:36.760 --> 00:30:40.680
So I'd like to just sort of say
thank you to MIT as a concept.

00:30:49.024 --> 00:31:01.030
[INAUDIBLE]

00:31:01.030 --> 00:31:03.970
If the groove's
going [BEAT BOXING],,

00:31:03.970 --> 00:31:09.320
and the bass is going
[BEAT BOXING WITH BASS NOTES],,

00:31:09.320 --> 00:31:10.340
that's cool, right?

00:31:10.340 --> 00:31:13.400
It's like, it's more cool
than [BASIC BASS LINE]..

00:31:13.400 --> 00:31:17.040
Yeah, [COMPLEX BEAT BOXING],,
because they

00:31:17.040 --> 00:31:19.040
have a gravity that goes
towards the same place,

00:31:19.040 --> 00:31:20.750
but they don't
have a unison line.

00:31:20.750 --> 00:31:21.980
Let's try a different groove.

00:31:21.980 --> 00:31:23.570
This is a groove
that's hard to nail,

00:31:23.570 --> 00:31:25.350
but it's the groove
on the record.

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:27.230
And it's like a lilt groove.

00:31:27.230 --> 00:31:29.811
So it's less of a shuffle.

00:31:29.811 --> 00:31:32.310
And it's like halfway between
a shuffle and a normal groove.

00:31:32.310 --> 00:31:37.270
So this is straight, and
then this is triplet.

00:31:37.270 --> 00:31:41.380
It's like halfway
between like this.

00:31:41.380 --> 00:31:43.479
It's less about putting
the high hat later.

00:31:43.479 --> 00:31:45.270
It's more about bringing
the snare earlier.

00:31:45.270 --> 00:31:45.853
So it's like--

00:31:45.853 --> 00:31:49.940
[BEAT BOXING]

00:31:49.940 --> 00:31:52.814
Actually, if I play and
then you guys can join in,

00:31:52.814 --> 00:31:54.480
maybe that's a good
way to get the feel.

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:55.063
So it's like--

00:32:22.030 --> 00:32:23.617
I dig it.

00:32:23.617 --> 00:32:24.950
Can I have a go at the drum kit?

00:32:24.950 --> 00:32:28.870
[INAUDIBLE]

00:32:28.870 --> 00:32:31.810
[PLAYING DRUMS]

00:32:44.060 --> 00:32:47.560
He very carefully and
deliberately swings it

00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:49.860
in certain ways and
modulates the time

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:51.390
and pulls and
pushes in a way that

00:32:51.390 --> 00:32:55.370
just gets this like
really visceral groove

00:32:55.370 --> 00:32:58.020
where you just like
get into it so much.

00:32:58.020 --> 00:33:01.830
[SINGING]

00:33:01.830 --> 00:33:04.950
That little bit of extra
effort to, you know, change it

00:33:04.950 --> 00:33:06.880
makes such a difference.

00:33:06.880 --> 00:33:09.795
And that's such a Jacob
kind of construct.

00:33:09.795 --> 00:33:12.080
It's like that extra
bit of intention

00:33:12.080 --> 00:33:14.420
that just-- you know, even
just a tiny little breath

00:33:14.420 --> 00:33:17.365
or a tiny little extra note or
something that doesn't quite,

00:33:17.365 --> 00:33:20.870
you know, fit in what the rules
say they're supposed to do.

00:33:20.870 --> 00:33:22.609
So at the end, were you guys--

00:33:22.609 --> 00:33:24.150
had you guys split
into two sections,

00:33:24.150 --> 00:33:25.310
