00:36:39.123 --> 00:36:41.015
Would you tell me what
we're searching for?

00:36:41.015 --> 00:36:41.961
All right.

00:36:41.961 --> 00:36:44.990
Say you don't want
to be my savior.

00:36:44.990 --> 00:36:48.420
You don't want to
close that door.

00:36:48.420 --> 00:36:49.539
I don't wanna hear a---

00:36:56.026 --> 00:36:58.521
I don't wanna be a preacher,
I just wanna be a man,

00:36:58.521 --> 00:37:01.515
I won't let you exaltate me.

00:37:01.515 --> 00:37:04.509
Just to keep me as I am.

00:37:04.509 --> 00:37:07.004
I don't want to be a preacher.

00:37:07.004 --> 00:37:08.002
I don't want to.

00:37:17.483 --> 00:37:21.475
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:37:27.990 --> 00:37:31.780
He's one of those once in
a lifetime kind of people

00:37:31.780 --> 00:37:33.949
that changes the way
you look at things.

00:37:42.432 --> 00:38:13.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:38:13.880 --> 00:38:15.300
It's really inspiring.

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:18.637
It makes me think that there's
real divinity in the world.

00:38:18.637 --> 00:38:21.619
[CROWD SINGING]

00:38:43.487 --> 00:38:49.040
Black bird singing
in the dead of night.

00:38:49.040 --> 00:38:52.990
Take these broken
wings and learn to fly.

00:38:52.990 --> 00:38:58.710
Oh, no, in all your
life, oh, no, oh,

00:38:58.710 --> 00:39:02.480
you were only waiting
for this moment

00:39:02.480 --> 00:39:04.895
to arrive, for
this moment to a--

00:39:04.895 --> 00:39:07.997
birds singing in the
dead of night, oh.

00:39:07.997 --> 00:39:10.319
Ba ba ba ba ba ba.

00:39:10.319 --> 00:39:14.950
Take these sunken eyes and
learn to see, learn to see,

00:39:14.950 --> 00:39:16.145
learn to see.

00:39:16.145 --> 00:39:23.930
All your life, oh, oh, you were
only waiting for this moment

00:39:23.930 --> 00:39:25.509
to be free, moment to be free.

00:39:25.509 --> 00:39:27.065
[END PLAYBACK]

00:39:27.065 --> 00:39:30.977
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:43.220 --> 00:39:45.860
I'm so happy.

00:39:45.860 --> 00:39:48.440
And this is what
Jacob's spirit does.

00:39:48.440 --> 00:39:53.450
I mean, it's just, he makes
you happy in so many deep ways.

00:39:53.450 --> 00:39:56.450
Can I just got a quick show
of hands of how many people

00:39:56.450 --> 00:39:58.460
were involved in
that whole residency?

00:39:58.460 --> 00:40:02.060
Whether you're a musician, or
you were behind the scenes,

00:40:02.060 --> 00:40:02.930
raise your hand.

00:40:02.930 --> 00:40:06.405
If you were involved in some
way or another, raise it high.

00:40:06.405 --> 00:40:09.800
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:14.650 --> 00:40:15.700
Thank you so much.

00:40:15.700 --> 00:40:18.100
Because this really
started as a dream

00:40:18.100 --> 00:40:19.970
that grew, and grew, and grew.

00:40:19.970 --> 00:40:21.310
And it was fabulous.

00:40:21.310 --> 00:40:23.560
We're going to give
the audience a chance

00:40:23.560 --> 00:40:26.440
to ask questions of all
these amazing people.

00:40:26.440 --> 00:40:28.330
But I want to say one thing.

00:40:28.330 --> 00:40:31.780
I've always been in awe of
the ephemeral nature of music,

00:40:31.780 --> 00:40:33.700
of live performance.

00:40:33.700 --> 00:40:36.100
Because once you
do it, it's done.

00:40:36.100 --> 00:40:39.160
You can never recapture it
quite the same way again.

00:40:39.160 --> 00:40:41.860
And this always
makes me exhilarated.

00:40:41.860 --> 00:40:44.620
But it always makes me
sad, so that what I find

00:40:44.620 --> 00:40:46.900
is that, after
really good concerts,

00:40:46.900 --> 00:40:50.890
I'm rather depressed for a
while, usually an hour or two.

00:40:50.890 --> 00:40:54.970
After this concert,
weeks I was depressed.

00:40:54.970 --> 00:41:00.110
So I thank you all for that
great feeling of depression.

00:41:00.110 --> 00:41:03.080
We're going to do this Q&amp;A.
And I think you know everybody.

00:41:03.080 --> 00:41:04.360
But I just want to introduce.

00:41:04.360 --> 00:41:08.200
First and foremost, here is
Jamshied Sharifi, class of '83.

00:41:08.200 --> 00:41:11.068
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:18.115 --> 00:41:19.740
We might get some
questions about this.

00:41:19.740 --> 00:41:23.310
But Jamshied is a fantastic
composer, and arranger,

00:41:23.310 --> 00:41:24.150
and producer.

00:41:24.150 --> 00:41:28.290
He was the Director of the
MIT Festival Jazz Ensemble

00:41:28.290 --> 00:41:31.980
for seven years, took it
on after he graduated.

00:41:31.980 --> 00:41:33.480
And he had the incredible--

00:41:33.480 --> 00:41:34.896
I still don't know
how he did it--

00:41:34.896 --> 00:41:37.230
task of not only
transcribing Jacob's music,

00:41:37.230 --> 00:41:39.435
but arranging it for
very large forces.

00:41:39.435 --> 00:41:42.384
So Jamshied Sharifi, yes.

00:41:42.384 --> 00:41:47.490
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:47.490 --> 00:41:50.530
Of course, you've
already met Jean Dunoyer,

00:41:50.530 --> 00:41:53.100
our wonderful film
director and editor.

00:41:53.100 --> 00:41:56.495
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:59.890 --> 00:42:02.390
And of course, the brilliant
and ever innovative

00:42:02.390 --> 00:42:06.140
media mastermind of MIT and
well beyond, Ben Bloomberg.

00:42:06.140 --> 00:42:09.633
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:18.620 --> 00:42:20.780
And of course, the
magical Jacob Collier,

00:42:20.780 --> 00:42:25.280
whose capacity for creativity
with heart knows no limits,

00:42:25.280 --> 00:42:26.610
Jacob.

00:42:26.610 --> 00:42:30.005
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:37.280 --> 00:42:38.300
So here's your chance.

00:42:38.300 --> 00:42:41.060
Anybody has a question,
we just ask you come down.

00:42:41.060 --> 00:42:42.620
There's a mic on either side.

00:42:42.620 --> 00:42:46.490
Any question of any of these
folks would be great to have.

00:42:54.150 --> 00:42:58.600
Hi, I have a really
exciting editing question,

00:42:58.600 --> 00:43:02.890
if there's such a thing, for
Jacob and for the filmmaker.

00:43:02.890 --> 00:43:05.140
How do you take
something like all

00:43:05.140 --> 00:43:10.450
that happened to be
concise as a filmmaker

00:43:10.450 --> 00:43:14.410
and not make it a Peter Jackson
Lord of the Rings movie with 19

00:43:14.410 --> 00:43:15.340
endings?

00:43:15.340 --> 00:43:18.550
And is there a part
of it like letting go?

00:43:18.550 --> 00:43:21.730
And also, I guess with
arranging your music

00:43:21.730 --> 00:43:23.710
with all these
people, and it could

00:43:23.710 --> 00:43:27.100
be like a 10 hour long
concert, so how much of it

00:43:27.100 --> 00:43:29.470
is letting go of
attachments and stuff

00:43:29.470 --> 00:43:32.150
like that to make the
whole thing better?

00:43:32.150 --> 00:43:35.660
All right, yeah, I can
handle that question.

00:43:35.660 --> 00:43:37.900
So really, what
it is is you spend

00:43:37.900 --> 00:43:39.580
a lot of time looking
at your footage.

00:43:39.580 --> 00:43:43.060
And you're looking
for the peaks,

00:43:43.060 --> 00:43:45.550
for the real moments
when everything's

00:43:45.550 --> 00:43:46.960
coalescing perfectly.

00:43:46.960 --> 00:43:48.460
And when you're
making film, there's

00:43:48.460 --> 00:43:50.950
always some things that
just fall off the edge, that

00:43:50.950 --> 00:43:52.660
just don't work at all.

00:43:52.660 --> 00:43:56.010
Or that great shot you
wanted just didn't happen.

00:43:56.010 --> 00:43:57.790
And so you really
look at your footage

00:43:57.790 --> 00:44:02.020
to concentrate the energy
on the bits that really fly.

00:44:02.020 --> 00:44:06.730
So in the case of the
rehearsal for example,

00:44:06.730 --> 00:44:10.570
I ended up boiling it down to
about 20 minutes of rehearsal.

00:44:10.570 --> 00:44:13.000
And I loved all of it.

00:44:13.000 --> 00:44:16.090
But once I get to
that point, then I've

00:44:16.090 --> 00:44:17.920
decided I like those 20 minutes.

00:44:17.920 --> 00:44:20.920
And then, I just
choose the bits that

00:44:20.920 --> 00:44:24.100
illustrate what might have
happened before and after,

00:44:24.100 --> 00:44:26.740
in terms of the song
that you're hearing

00:44:26.740 --> 00:44:29.030
before or after in performance.

00:44:29.030 --> 00:44:31.480
So those are some of
the thought processes

00:44:31.480 --> 00:44:33.420
that I go through with editing.

00:44:33.420 --> 00:44:36.250
It's really about
finding the nectar,

00:44:36.250 --> 00:44:38.125
squeezing it to the maximum.

00:44:38.125 --> 00:44:40.000
I don't know if Jacob
wants to add something.

00:44:40.000 --> 00:44:42.730
Yeah, I can relate
to that, for sure.

00:44:42.730 --> 00:44:45.190
I think for me, when it
comes to making music,

00:44:45.190 --> 00:44:49.290
there's always the sorrow of
letting go of a good idea,

00:44:49.290 --> 00:44:49.790
right?

00:44:49.790 --> 00:44:52.150
It's just very sad.

00:44:52.150 --> 00:44:55.450
However, I've always had
this idea, a slight mechanism

00:44:55.450 --> 00:44:56.950
for dealing with
this kind of thing,

00:44:56.950 --> 00:45:00.220
where I often record
ideas on voice memos.

00:45:00.220 --> 00:45:01.248
So it's like

00:45:01.248 --> 00:45:02.622
[BEAT BOXING]

00:45:02.622 --> 00:45:04.870
And I whisper to myself
about how to create,

00:45:04.870 --> 00:45:07.650
how to manifest it from that
point and all sorts of things.

00:45:07.650 --> 00:45:10.005
I then take that idea and
put it in a timeline view.

00:45:10.005 --> 00:45:12.040
I then cut it up
and color code it

00:45:12.040 --> 00:45:14.390
in gold, silver,
and bronze ideas.

00:45:14.390 --> 00:45:17.259
And bronze ideas are filler.

00:45:17.259 --> 00:45:19.300
And in my case, those are
sometimes quite extreme

00:45:19.300 --> 00:45:20.644
already.

00:45:20.644 --> 00:45:22.560
Silver ideas are the
moments that make you go,

00:45:22.560 --> 00:45:23.351
whoa, that's crazy.

00:45:23.351 --> 00:45:26.220
And gold moments are
like mic drop moments.

00:45:26.220 --> 00:45:30.820
And my challenge is always
to have the patience

00:45:30.820 --> 00:45:34.090
and trust the listener to leave
the gold ideas to the end.

00:45:34.090 --> 00:45:36.800
Because I want to
do them all at once.

00:45:36.800 --> 00:45:39.190
And I've always had a love.

00:45:39.190 --> 00:45:42.220
And my intuition has always
found that doing everything

00:45:42.220 --> 00:45:43.570
at once is really fun.

00:45:43.570 --> 00:45:46.300
And the older I get--

00:45:46.300 --> 00:45:48.472
now, I'm all grown up.

00:45:48.472 --> 00:45:53.050
Yeah, and I think I'm
learning that it's

00:45:53.050 --> 00:45:55.270
that tricky thing of, the
more you know how to do,

00:45:55.270 --> 00:45:58.100
the more courage it takes
not to do it all at once.

00:45:58.100 --> 00:46:03.340
But the more you have that said
courage, the greater the depth

00:46:03.340 --> 00:46:05.050
of the end result of something.

00:46:05.050 --> 00:46:08.620
So I think it's a process of
understanding your own process.

00:46:08.620 --> 00:46:10.930
For me, it's about letting
everything come out.

00:46:10.930 --> 00:46:13.690
Everything has to come out, all
the ideas, all of the visions,

00:46:13.690 --> 00:46:16.300
all of the various
things that one juggles

00:46:16.300 --> 00:46:17.680
in the creative process.

00:46:17.680 --> 00:46:19.840
I think that, if you
don't let that come out,

00:46:19.840 --> 00:46:21.850
then there's something
that is never

00:46:21.850 --> 00:46:27.310
going to be done as a result.
And so everything comes out.

00:46:27.310 --> 00:46:30.042
And then, one needs the
courage to deal with each thing

00:46:30.042 --> 00:46:32.500
as it is, and then to place it
where it needs to be placed,

00:46:32.500 --> 00:46:33.570
if that makes any sense.

00:46:33.570 --> 00:46:35.920
Jacob, given
whatever preconceived

00:46:35.920 --> 00:46:38.020
notions you had about MIT--

00:46:38.020 --> 00:46:41.320
and we all had them before
we came and discovered

00:46:41.320 --> 00:46:42.760
what MIT is all about--

00:46:42.760 --> 00:46:44.530
given those
preconceived notions,

00:46:44.530 --> 00:46:46.780
what surprised you
most about interacting

00:46:46.780 --> 00:46:48.746
with these wonderful students?

00:46:48.746 --> 00:46:49.870
Yeah, fantastic question.

00:46:49.870 --> 00:46:53.860
So I mean, I feel like I had
scratched the surface of what

00:46:53.860 --> 00:46:54.610
MIT was all about.

00:46:54.610 --> 00:46:57.110
Because I've been hanging
with Ben for a while,

00:46:57.110 --> 00:46:59.680
I guess a year,
if that's a while.

00:46:59.680 --> 00:47:00.190
Two years.

00:47:00.190 --> 00:47:01.540
Two years.

00:47:01.540 --> 00:47:05.980
And I think I was
expecting some fun.

00:47:05.980 --> 00:47:08.690
And I was expecting
some innovation.

00:47:08.690 --> 00:47:13.390
But the quality of the
brilliance of the minds

00:47:13.390 --> 00:47:14.540
just blew me away.

00:47:14.540 --> 00:47:17.990
The moment I came
in in September,

00:47:17.990 --> 00:47:19.750
and dipped my toe in,
I think that's where

00:47:19.750 --> 00:47:21.250
we got some of the
rehearsal footage

00:47:21.250 --> 00:47:23.680
was from that initial
rehearsal time.

00:47:23.680 --> 00:47:25.780
And it's like, these
guys are just alive.

00:47:25.780 --> 00:47:29.860
And when you look someone in the
eye and their eyes are going--

00:47:29.860 --> 00:47:32.780
it's just that I connect
with that so much.

00:47:32.780 --> 00:47:34.620
And it's wonderful.

00:47:34.620 --> 00:47:37.510
And often with collaborations,
and especially with teaching,

00:47:37.510 --> 00:47:39.910
you come to these
institutions to teach,

00:47:39.910 --> 00:47:42.010
whatever that happens
to mean, and there

00:47:42.010 --> 00:47:46.189
are these students with no eyes
looking at you in the audience.

00:47:46.189 --> 00:47:47.980
But here, it's like
everyone is just ready.

00:47:47.980 --> 00:47:49.271
They're just thirsty and ready.

00:47:49.271 --> 00:47:50.860
And they're like sponges.

00:47:50.860 --> 00:47:54.370
So you say an idea once and
it sticks with everybody.

00:47:54.370 --> 00:47:55.280
And that's so rare.

00:47:55.280 --> 00:47:57.490
I can't tell you
how rare that is.

00:47:57.490 --> 00:48:00.430
And then, obviously, when
you rehearse, it's exciting.

00:48:00.430 --> 00:48:02.440
And when you arrange and
discuss, it's exciting.

00:48:02.440 --> 00:48:04.810
But when everyone
gets on that stage,

00:48:04.810 --> 00:48:07.040
no one really knows
what's going to happen.

00:48:07.040 --> 00:48:09.610
Me and Fred had discussed at
length, the different sections,

00:48:09.610 --> 00:48:11.410
and the order of the
songs, and things.

00:48:11.410 --> 00:48:13.712
But things change so fast.

00:48:13.712 --> 00:48:15.170
And then, you're
taken by surprise.

00:48:15.170 --> 00:48:20.020
And I think, for a collective
group of people who are perhaps

00:48:20.020 --> 00:48:22.510
used to organizing
things, and frameworks

00:48:22.510 --> 00:48:25.390
and understanding that things
happen in a certain order,

00:48:25.390 --> 00:48:28.690
and these are the
greatest science and math

00:48:28.690 --> 00:48:31.030
brains of our generation.

00:48:31.030 --> 00:48:34.895
But then, you could drop a
bomb and people all respond.

00:48:34.895 --> 00:48:37.270
And I think that was something
I wasn't expecting at all,

00:48:37.270 --> 00:48:39.023
but something I
was elated to find.

00:48:39.023 --> 00:48:40.472
Thank you.

00:48:40.472 --> 00:48:41.438
You're welcome.

00:48:44.336 --> 00:48:46.500
This is for Jacob.

00:48:46.500 --> 00:48:49.880
So you've obviously used
extensive music technology

00:48:49.880 --> 00:48:52.230
and you're here because you're
pushing the limits of it.

00:48:52.230 --> 00:48:56.010
What kind of music technology
do you have musical visions

00:48:56.010 --> 00:48:58.200
for in the distant
future, for the tech

00:48:58.200 --> 00:49:00.340
that's like not even close?

00:49:00.340 --> 00:49:05.690
And also second question, do you
want to grab a pint after this?

00:49:05.690 --> 00:49:06.918
On me.

00:49:06.918 --> 00:49:11.040
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:11.040 --> 00:49:12.140
Thank you very much.

00:49:12.140 --> 00:49:16.125
Yeah, to the latter is a yes.

00:49:16.125 --> 00:49:17.810
All right, see you there.

00:49:17.810 --> 00:49:20.640
To the former, I guess Ben
and I can answer this question

00:49:20.640 --> 00:49:22.091
together.

00:49:22.091 --> 00:49:24.090
Yeah, so when Ben sent
me this Facebook message,

00:49:24.090 --> 00:49:26.600
it was very thrilling for me.

00:49:26.600 --> 00:49:28.080
And actually, it
was serendipitous

00:49:28.080 --> 00:49:28.871
that I even saw it.

00:49:28.871 --> 00:49:33.399
Because I tend to stay
clear of Facebook messages.

00:49:33.399 --> 00:49:34.440
Because it's a minefield.

00:49:34.440 --> 00:49:36.709
You never know what weird
stuff you're going to find.

00:49:36.709 --> 00:49:38.250
And it just so
happened that, one day

00:49:38.250 --> 00:49:41.110
out of three or four weeks,
I decided to check them out.

00:49:41.110 --> 00:49:43.920
And on the top of the
pile was Ben Bloomberg.

00:49:43.920 --> 00:49:46.084
And so I clicked on his name.

00:49:46.084 --> 00:49:48.000
And then, there was
this-- it wasn't long-- it

00:49:48.000 --> 00:49:49.270
was a short paragraph.

00:49:49.270 --> 00:49:49.770
It basically just said--

00:49:49.770 --> 00:49:51.478
I mean, you can say
it better than I can.

00:49:51.478 --> 00:49:54.530
But it basically just said,
I've just seen a video of yours.

00:49:54.530 --> 00:49:56.280
And I think it's exciting.

00:49:56.280 --> 00:49:58.020
And I just create things.

00:49:58.020 --> 00:49:59.420
I love to create things.

00:49:59.420 --> 00:50:01.530
And to me, that was like,
well, that's me as well.

00:50:01.530 --> 00:50:03.600
I love to create things.

00:50:03.600 --> 00:50:07.790
But Ben likes to create digital,
performance-flavored things.

00:50:07.790 --> 00:50:10.620
And yeah, I guess as you saw in
the documentary, it was like,

00:50:10.620 --> 00:50:12.280
do you want to make anything?

00:50:12.280 --> 00:50:14.770
And the answer to that
was an absolute yes.

00:50:14.770 --> 00:50:20.100
And we jumped on Skype
and spoke for three hours.

00:50:20.100 --> 00:50:22.020
And it was just like--

00:50:22.020 --> 00:50:24.660
I'd been waiting-- how old
was I at the time, 19 or 20?

00:50:24.660 --> 00:50:28.020
I'd been waiting 20 years
for that conversation.

00:50:28.020 --> 00:50:30.000
So it was like--

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:32.500
And Ben was just it
was just astounding.

00:50:32.500 --> 00:50:34.530
Because it would be
very easy just to say,

00:50:34.530 --> 00:50:40.600
well, that's a lot of stuff,
and that was fun, goodbye,

00:50:40.600 --> 00:50:42.310
that sort of thing.

00:50:42.310 --> 00:50:44.850
But I think, partly
because a lot of the stuff

00:50:44.850 --> 00:50:48.480
just wasn't really
possible, and some of it

00:50:48.480 --> 00:50:50.070
still is not
possible, and some of

00:50:50.070 --> 00:50:54.750
has been made possible by
Ben, which is just astounding.

00:50:54.750 --> 00:50:57.420
And I guess the first thing, I
wanted to tour my room, right?

00:50:57.420 --> 00:50:59.141
I wanted to tour my
room, which is not

00:50:59.141 --> 00:51:00.390
a conventional way of touring.

00:51:00.390 --> 00:51:02.760
Because normally, one
would tour with a band.

00:51:02.760 --> 00:51:04.593
But I want to be able
to tour with the room.

00:51:04.593 --> 00:51:06.997
And I wanted to get to sing
in harmony on the spot.

00:51:06.997 --> 00:51:09.080
Do you want to explain a
bit about the harmonizer,

00:51:09.080 --> 00:51:12.150
and how we did it, and how it
works, and that whole shebang?

00:51:12.150 --> 00:51:18.440
Yeah, I mean, so it's all of
our favorite sounds in one box.

00:51:18.440 --> 00:51:23.240
And there are a lot of other
approaches to similar things,

00:51:23.240 --> 00:51:26.230
like phase vocodors and all
kinds of things out there.

00:51:26.230 --> 00:51:30.020
But first thing Jacob said
is, well, all the ones

00:51:30.020 --> 00:51:32.210
I could find only
have four notes.

00:51:32.210 --> 00:51:36.710
And I want to play way more
than four notes at a time.

00:51:36.710 --> 00:51:38.810
And so the first
thing we did is we

00:51:38.810 --> 00:51:40.940
started to try to
figure out how to make

00:51:40.940 --> 00:51:43.460
one that could play a
gazillion notes simultaneously.

00:51:43.460 --> 00:51:46.576
And it was like, well,
what if I play a chord,

00:51:46.576 --> 00:51:48.200
and then I want to
keep that one going,

00:51:48.200 --> 00:51:49.949
and then play something
else on top of it?

00:51:49.949 --> 00:51:54.200
And so we came up with
this thing, actually.

00:51:54.200 --> 00:51:57.200
It's right there, which
is a bunch of electronics,

00:51:57.200 --> 00:52:00.440
and software, and some
off-the-shelf stuff,

00:52:00.440 --> 00:52:01.870
and some custom stuff.

00:52:01.870 --> 00:52:08.010
And yeah, I guess that was the
first thing we sat together.

00:52:08.010 --> 00:52:10.580
We did in about a weekend.

00:52:10.580 --> 00:52:11.890
Jacob came to Boston to visit.

00:52:11.890 --> 00:52:13.015
And we put it all together.

00:52:13.015 --> 00:52:16.570
And then, we started
thinking about other stuff.

00:52:16.570 --> 00:52:19.250
Yeah, I mean, it's
an ongoing challenge.

00:52:19.250 --> 00:52:22.700
It's like, as a human
being, how can I

00:52:22.700 --> 00:52:25.280
make my creative process
bigger without detracting

00:52:25.280 --> 00:52:26.300
from my humanity?

00:52:26.300 --> 00:52:27.424
That's the thing, isn't it?

00:52:27.424 --> 00:52:30.200
Because technology just
steals humanity sometimes,

00:52:30.200 --> 00:52:33.150
stows it away in some
invisible, dangerous place.

00:52:33.150 --> 00:52:36.500
And I think for me,
it's that thing of,

00:52:36.500 --> 00:52:40.565
if I had 11 arms or 12
arms, I would try and play

00:52:40.565 --> 00:52:41.690
all the instruments at one.

00:52:41.690 --> 00:52:42.190
But I don't.

00:52:42.190 --> 00:52:44.810
So the only way to do it is to
try and use our imaginations

00:52:44.810 --> 00:52:45.893
about how to achieve that.

00:52:45.893 --> 00:52:51.070
But the goal is like more human,
rather than remove from human.

00:52:51.070 --> 00:52:55.460
And I think that's an aesthetic
that we said and share.

00:52:55.460 --> 00:52:58.360
Moving forward,
there's so much to do

00:52:58.360 --> 00:52:59.750
in the world of technology.

00:52:59.750 --> 00:53:03.770
But it has to feel like you
can touch it, you can feel it.

00:53:03.770 --> 00:53:06.771
And the moment you touch,
it gets you out of yourself.

00:53:06.771 --> 00:53:09.020
Because for me, those are
the best creative processes.

00:53:09.020 --> 00:53:11.210
It doesn't matter whether
it's the piano, or a bass,

00:53:11.210 --> 00:53:12.580
or a computer, or something.

00:53:12.580 --> 00:53:15.870
It's like, if you
touch it and it goes--

00:53:15.870 --> 00:53:17.510
then, you want to
create into it.

00:53:17.510 --> 00:53:20.474
And so yeah, I mean, we've got
a bunch of strange projects

00:53:20.474 --> 00:53:22.640
that are flying around,
like this is the harmonizer.

00:53:22.640 --> 00:53:25.250
And there's now soon
to come to vocalizer,

00:53:25.250 --> 00:53:28.475
which is a dangerous
sample library made

00:53:28.475 --> 00:53:29.636
of voices that does--

00:53:29.636 --> 00:53:31.860
and various other things.

00:53:31.860 --> 00:53:33.800
And then, we're
trying to figure out

00:53:33.800 --> 00:53:37.670
a way to essentially
teach a computer how

00:53:37.670 --> 00:53:42.530
to navigate harmony, and
then present to humans

00:53:42.530 --> 00:53:44.150
the user interface
with which they

00:53:44.150 --> 00:53:46.910
can interact using their own
emotional criteria to navigate.

00:53:46.910 --> 00:53:49.400
So they don't have to be
musicians to know that,

00:53:49.400 --> 00:53:50.945
if you add a

00:53:50.945 --> 00:53:52.266
[PLAYS CHORD]

00:53:52.266 --> 00:53:56.180
To that chord, it
warms your heart up.

00:53:56.180 --> 00:53:58.212
But if I can just put
that in someone's hand,

00:53:58.212 --> 00:54:00.420
but they don't have to say,
OK, it's an A minor chord

00:54:00.420 --> 00:54:03.980
and I'm adding a D, they can
just say, I want to just go--

00:54:03.980 --> 00:54:05.890
and then, they can
make it happen.

00:54:05.890 --> 00:54:08.660
So it's a bit of
a romantic idea.

00:54:08.660 --> 00:54:10.750
But we're working towards
it piece by piece, where

00:54:10.750 --> 00:54:12.590
we are going to tackle that.

00:54:12.590 --> 00:54:15.500
I think is a challenge,
especially with the technology

00:54:15.500 --> 00:54:19.190
stuff, figuring out actually
what the tech is good for

00:54:19.190 --> 00:54:22.110
and what Jacob is
good for basically.

00:54:22.110 --> 00:54:24.800
And so a lot of people
build systems, especially

00:54:24.800 --> 00:54:26.300
for live performance.

00:54:26.300 --> 00:54:28.010
And you're trying to
lean on the computer

00:54:28.010 --> 00:54:30.429
to do something that
just a human is always

00:54:30.429 --> 00:54:31.220
going to do better.

00:54:31.220 --> 00:54:35.180
And maybe 10 years, 20
years down the road,

00:54:35.180 --> 00:54:36.690
we'll have something
that does it.

00:54:36.690 --> 00:54:38.550
But when you look
at it right now

00:54:38.550 --> 00:54:41.300
and you're thinking about
an experience for people

00:54:41.300 --> 00:54:46.700
in an audience, computers
do certain things

00:54:46.700 --> 00:54:48.810
really, really well.

00:54:48.810 --> 00:54:50.820
But we do certain things
really, really well.

00:54:50.820 --> 00:54:52.670
And so finding the
optimal synergy

00:54:52.670 --> 00:54:57.070
there is what we're
excited about working on.

00:54:57.070 --> 00:55:00.110
A computer is ever going
to follow a score as well

00:55:00.110 --> 00:55:04.030
as a human can, at least in
the next two and a half years,

00:55:04.030 --> 00:55:06.610
let's say.

00:55:06.610 --> 00:55:10.050
So maybe we'll wait
two and a half years.

00:55:10.050 --> 00:55:17.567
But those are a lot of the kinds
of decisions and philosophies

00:55:17.567 --> 00:55:18.400
that we think about.

00:55:21.064 --> 00:55:23.170
Where can we apply the
technology in a way

00:55:23.170 --> 00:55:27.490
where it's actually doing
something that helps,

00:55:27.490 --> 00:55:29.500
that makes that
performance more musical?

00:55:32.330 --> 00:55:36.410
So your anecdote with the vacuum
cleaner reminded me of this.

00:55:36.410 --> 00:55:40.220
So in pieces like "In the Bleak
Midwinter" and "Fascinating

00:55:40.220 --> 00:55:44.120
Rhythm," you display this
microtonally perfect sense

00:55:44.120 --> 00:55:44.841
of perfect pitch.

00:55:44.841 --> 00:55:47.090
How much of that was just
something you've always had?

00:55:47.090 --> 00:55:49.836
And how much of
that was developed?

00:55:49.836 --> 00:55:50.710
Interesting question.

00:55:50.710 --> 00:55:56.990
So perfect pitch is just
a kind of memory, really.

00:55:56.990 --> 00:56:00.740
It's being able
to recall a sound.

00:56:00.740 --> 00:56:04.310
And I suppose the more I've
recorded things over the years,

00:56:04.310 --> 00:56:07.150
the more I've realize
that this is not in tune.

00:56:07.150 --> 00:56:08.700
It's not in tune at all.

00:56:08.700 --> 00:56:11.065
To very quick demonstrate,
if you take a major triad--

00:56:11.065 --> 00:56:12.190
I mean, you guys know this.

00:56:12.190 --> 00:56:14.773
Because this is like Pythagoras
figured this stuff out, right?

00:56:14.773 --> 00:56:19.330
But on the piano, this
is a G major chord.

00:56:19.330 --> 00:56:22.250
And the major third is
actually 14 cents too high,

00:56:22.250 --> 00:56:25.690
which means 1,400ths
of a semitone.

00:56:25.690 --> 00:56:28.200
So the correct one is like--

00:56:28.200 --> 00:56:31.050
[SINGS NOTE]

00:56:31.050 --> 00:56:32.250
It's like that, out of tune.

00:56:32.250 --> 00:56:34.250
But I realized this.

00:56:34.250 --> 00:56:36.750
I mean, it's like, how does
no one know this stuff, man?

00:56:36.750 --> 00:56:37.260
It's crazy.

00:56:37.260 --> 00:56:40.050
It's like a hoax.

00:56:40.050 --> 00:56:43.940
And so I just strive
to sing more in tune.

00:56:43.940 --> 00:56:47.370
And I had to disregard computers
knowing better than I did.

00:56:47.370 --> 00:56:52.322
Because I had to learn about
how to tune chords by ear.

00:56:52.322 --> 00:56:53.780
And then suddenly,
it's like, well,

00:56:53.780 --> 00:56:57.572
my perfect pitch declines
that B. Because it's not A440.

00:56:57.572 --> 00:56:59.280
And then suddenly,
you learn that there's

00:56:59.280 --> 00:57:00.470
no reason why 12 keys are.

00:57:00.470 --> 00:57:01.570
They're just this limit.

00:57:01.570 --> 00:57:03.027
There are more.

00:57:03.027 --> 00:57:05.610
And then yeah, so for example,
"In the Bleak Midwinter," where

00:57:05.610 --> 00:57:07.710
go to G half-sharp
in the fourth verse,

00:57:07.710 --> 00:57:09.780
it's just a voyage
into a key that

00:57:09.780 --> 00:57:12.354
is slightly fresher
to everybody's ears,

00:57:12.354 --> 00:57:13.020
definitely mine.

00:57:13.020 --> 00:57:14.645
Because I got bored
of the normal ones.

00:57:14.645 --> 00:57:16.980
So I thought it would be
fun to try things out.

00:57:16.980 --> 00:57:17.790
But you know what?

00:57:17.790 --> 00:57:20.189
I think anybody
with a skill set--

00:57:20.189 --> 00:57:21.730
I'm sure you guys
can relate to this.

00:57:21.730 --> 00:57:26.401
But you have your skills that
you use on a regular basis.

00:57:26.401 --> 00:57:27.900
And then, once those
get tired, it's

00:57:27.900 --> 00:57:30.870
to do with knowing how to
gather in a way that's not

00:57:30.870 --> 00:57:33.360
going to disrupt what you
already have but is going

00:57:33.360 --> 00:57:36.330
to push you and stretch
your imagination

00:57:36.330 --> 00:57:38.190
and your creative strengths.

00:57:38.190 --> 00:57:40.890
And I just think, for me, that
the microtonal thing was a bit

00:57:40.890 --> 00:57:42.220
of a game that, for a while--

00:57:42.220 --> 00:57:43.360
I'm still into it.

00:57:43.360 --> 00:57:45.810
But there was a while
where I was really into it,

00:57:45.810 --> 00:57:46.620
and voices going

00:57:46.620 --> 00:57:48.380
[SINGING NOTES]

00:57:48.380 --> 00:57:48.940
Sort of--

00:57:48.940 --> 00:57:49.280
[SINGING NOTES]

00:57:49.280 --> 00:57:50.760
It was just interesting for me.

00:57:50.760 --> 00:57:53.020
Because voices need to move.

00:57:53.020 --> 00:57:54.430
That's it.

00:57:54.430 --> 00:57:56.544
That's the gesture,
movement and arrival.

00:57:56.544 --> 00:57:58.210
And if you arrive in
a satisfying place,

00:57:58.210 --> 00:57:59.710
it doesn't matter
how you get there.

00:57:59.710 --> 00:58:02.070
So microtones or no microtones,
it still does the job.

00:58:02.070 --> 00:58:04.290
So it's just a nice
thing to think about.

00:58:04.290 --> 00:58:06.530
It's another example
of just thinking

00:58:06.530 --> 00:58:09.330
in between the gaps of details
and finding that there's

00:58:09.330 --> 00:58:12.210
magic in the things that
aren't quantized, by which

00:58:12.210 --> 00:58:14.040
I mean everything's the same.

00:58:14.040 --> 00:58:17.320
Like, if I play a groove
into Logic that's like this.

00:58:17.320 --> 00:58:20.760
And then, I quantize
it and it goes--

00:58:20.760 --> 00:58:23.400
it's fine, and it's
accurate, and techno guys are

00:58:23.400 --> 00:58:25.730
going to dance well to that.

00:58:25.730 --> 00:58:29.159
But it goes back
to your question.

00:58:29.159 --> 00:58:30.450
It's lost some of its humanity.

00:58:30.450 --> 00:58:32.741
Because the computer squeezed
the humanity out of that.

00:58:32.741 --> 00:58:35.160
So Samba, for example--

00:58:35.160 --> 00:58:38.490
[BEAT BOXING]

00:58:38.490 --> 00:58:39.450
The groove there--

00:58:39.450 --> 00:58:42.030
[BEAT BOXING]

00:58:42.030 --> 00:58:42.960
It's not straight.

00:58:42.960 --> 00:58:44.890
It rolls like an egg.

00:58:44.890 --> 00:58:47.070
And once you get to learn
that, it's like, oh,

00:58:47.070 --> 00:58:49.110
so that's not accurate then.

00:58:49.110 --> 00:58:50.670
This is just one
potential thing.

00:58:50.670 --> 00:58:52.200
All the semitones don't
have to be the same size.

00:58:52.200 --> 00:58:53.116
You can go in between.

00:58:53.116 --> 00:58:54.600
And you can stretch
things around.

00:58:54.600 --> 00:58:56.850
Yeah, I mean, I could talk
for a long time about this.

00:58:56.850 --> 00:59:01.230
But the basic premise is that
things that are perfect just

00:59:01.230 --> 00:59:05.580
generally are neither
perfect nor moving.

00:59:05.580 --> 00:59:08.700
And so it's just nice to
push, as much as you can,

00:59:08.700 --> 00:59:11.640
the things that we believe
to be straight and correct

00:59:11.640 --> 00:59:14.580
into places that have
a bit more potential

00:59:14.580 --> 00:59:16.760
for unfamiliar,
emotional language,

00:59:16.760 --> 00:59:18.442
if that makes any sense.

00:59:21.760 --> 00:59:25.090
One question for
Jamshied, if I might.

00:59:25.090 --> 00:59:28.860
I think that you might be the
only person, other than Jacob,

00:59:28.860 --> 00:59:33.480
with Hajanga, who has arranged
Jacob's own music for forces

00:59:33.480 --> 00:59:36.214
beyond just Jacob alone.

00:59:36.214 --> 00:59:37.880
I don't know if that's
true, but anyway.

00:59:37.880 --> 00:59:38.810
It might be close to being true.

00:59:38.810 --> 00:59:40.420
I think it's close
to being true.

00:59:40.420 --> 00:59:43.850
So how was that process?

00:59:43.850 --> 00:59:45.250
You actually know.

00:59:45.250 --> 00:59:45.750
I do.

00:59:45.750 --> 00:59:49.170
But they don't.

00:59:49.170 --> 00:59:55.500
When I started on the business
of arranging Jacob's music,

00:59:55.500 --> 00:59:58.860
within about an hour, I
realized that I was not

00:59:58.860 --> 01:00:01.290
going to be able to
really understand

01:00:01.290 --> 01:00:03.190
what was going on inside of it.

01:00:03.190 --> 01:00:07.350
So I called up you guys and
asked for the multitracks,

01:00:07.350 --> 01:00:10.890
in other words for the
versions of the songs in which

01:00:10.890 --> 01:00:13.560
every element was isolated,
so that I could hear them

01:00:13.560 --> 01:00:16.510
in isolation and try to
figure out what was going on.

01:00:16.510 --> 01:00:20.490
And even with that,
I mean, Ben sent me

01:00:20.490 --> 01:00:23.280
sessions that were
about 60 tracks wide.

01:00:23.280 --> 01:00:24.704
But a single track would have--

01:00:24.704 --> 01:00:26.245
I mean, it would be
labeled "Guitar."

01:00:26.245 --> 01:00:31.180
And it would have
12 guitars in it.

01:00:31.180 --> 01:00:35.430
So it was an incredible
density in all of this.

01:00:35.430 --> 01:00:38.040
And I mean typically,
I'll spend two or three

01:00:38.040 --> 01:00:40.230
days on a good
sized arrangement,

01:00:40.230 --> 01:00:42.000
on an arrangement for orchestra.

01:00:42.000 --> 01:00:45.210
And all of these for Jacob where
at least eight or nine days.

01:00:45.210 --> 01:00:48.480
Because there was so much
decoding, and understanding,

01:00:48.480 --> 01:00:52.830
and really re-orienting
myself to what I thought

01:00:52.830 --> 01:00:53.820
I knew about music.

01:00:53.820 --> 01:00:54.840
Because you were doing--

01:00:54.840 --> 01:00:56.610
I mean, you're saying this.

01:00:56.610 --> 01:00:59.250
But I'm verifying
you're doing stuff

01:00:59.250 --> 01:01:02.730
that I've never seen before,
that I've never seen people

01:01:02.730 --> 01:01:05.640
consider, in terms of the way
harmonies are put together,

01:01:05.640 --> 01:01:09.240
in terms of the
way the time was.

01:01:09.240 --> 01:01:11.490
Because it definitely
wasn't quantized.

01:01:11.490 --> 01:01:13.430
And yet, it grooved its ass off.

01:01:13.430 --> 01:01:18.330
And I was trying to figure
out how is this all working.

01:01:18.330 --> 01:01:23.130
So yeah, there were
days of what I'll just

01:01:23.130 --> 01:01:25.200
call decoding and
analysis, of trying

01:01:25.200 --> 01:01:29.070
to understand where
it was coming from,

01:01:29.070 --> 01:01:34.530
and then taking that and putting
it on paper, so that us mortals

01:01:34.530 --> 01:01:37.782
could read it.

01:01:37.782 --> 01:01:39.720
It was revelatory.

01:01:39.720 --> 01:01:43.980
It was really enjoyable
and eye opening.

01:01:43.980 --> 01:01:47.950
But yeah, it was a revelation.

01:01:47.950 --> 01:01:54.310
I still feel that and feel
it more hearing pieces again.

01:01:54.310 --> 01:01:58.230
There's so much in
what you're doing

01:01:58.230 --> 01:02:02.730
that it's at a level
I've never seen before.

01:02:02.730 --> 01:02:05.540
So I don't know if that
answers your question.

01:02:05.540 --> 01:02:09.860
[APPLAUSE]

01:02:09.860 --> 01:02:11.127
Thank you so much.

01:02:11.127 --> 01:02:12.710
We'll have one more
question, I think.

01:02:12.710 --> 01:02:15.910
Hi, OK, so I was going to
preface this by asking you

01:02:15.910 --> 01:02:17.560
if you have any problems.

01:02:17.560 --> 01:02:20.380
But the real question
that I was going to ask

01:02:20.380 --> 01:02:27.240
is what obstacles
have you faced?

01:02:27.240 --> 01:02:29.610
Because I know I personally
suffer, in my artistry,

01:02:29.610 --> 01:02:34.460
from a lot of anxiety and a
lot of perfectionism paralysis

01:02:34.460 --> 01:02:34.960
things.

01:02:34.960 --> 01:02:37.602
It's just the philosophy
of, well, I mean,

01:02:37.602 --> 01:02:39.060
if you're not going
to do it right,

01:02:39.060 --> 01:02:40.800
then you shouldn't do it at all.

01:02:40.800 --> 01:02:45.360
Are there any major things
that you either are currently

01:02:45.360 --> 01:02:49.200
working through or you
have overcame to become

01:02:49.200 --> 01:02:53.050
such an expressive musician?

01:02:53.050 --> 01:02:55.220
That's a really wonderful
question, thank you,

01:02:55.220 --> 01:02:58.590
and a huge question as well.

01:02:58.590 --> 01:03:01.990
That's why I said, do you have
any problems would be the best.

01:03:01.990 --> 01:03:04.490
Do I have any problems?

01:03:04.490 --> 01:03:06.290
Yeah, so when I go to
restaurants and look

01:03:06.290 --> 01:03:09.340
at the menu, I cannot make a
decision about what to order.

01:03:09.340 --> 01:03:10.790
That's it.

01:03:10.790 --> 01:03:16.910
So I think, OK, to break this
down into short sections,

01:03:16.910 --> 01:03:19.760
one is to do with the
way that you learn

01:03:19.760 --> 01:03:21.460
and the reason
that you learn it.

01:03:21.460 --> 01:03:25.430
And for me, as basically
a self-taught musician,

01:03:25.430 --> 01:03:29.910
I didn't look to other
people for the answers.

01:03:29.910 --> 01:03:35.410
And so I think was brought up
to very much trust my intuition

01:03:35.410 --> 01:03:37.100
to lead me in the
right direction.

01:03:37.100 --> 01:03:40.450
And I think I enjoyed the
process of arriving at a place

01:03:40.450 --> 01:03:42.410
more than I did the place.

01:03:42.410 --> 01:03:48.190
Because when you get to a
place, it's like, oh, OK, done.

01:03:48.190 --> 01:03:51.010
And for me, that's like
what Fred was saying.

01:03:51.010 --> 01:03:52.780
It's like you do all
this glorious work

01:03:52.780 --> 01:03:53.530
to get to a place.

01:03:53.530 --> 01:03:54.270
And then, you do the show.

01:03:54.270 --> 01:03:54.910
And it's gone.

01:03:54.910 --> 01:03:56.209
And it's a little bit sad.

01:03:56.209 --> 01:03:58.750
So for me, in terms of learning
things, it's always just been

01:03:58.750 --> 01:04:02.620
about, instead of
isolating a destination,

01:04:02.620 --> 01:04:04.360
and then going there
and achieving it,

01:04:04.360 --> 01:04:07.240
and the achieving of it
being what keeps me alive,

01:04:07.240 --> 01:04:11.140
it's like the varying
forms of nourishment

01:04:11.140 --> 01:04:14.770
that I'm subject to during
the process of gathering

01:04:14.770 --> 01:04:18.310
a particular area within
a framework of something,

01:04:18.310 --> 01:04:21.290
that process is
something I really enjoy.

01:04:21.290 --> 01:04:23.210
But I never really
minded what other people

01:04:23.210 --> 01:04:24.550
thought of what I was doing.

01:04:24.550 --> 01:04:26.660
And I still don't
really mind, actually.

01:04:26.660 --> 01:04:30.800
And so the fact that
now I'm making music

01:04:30.800 --> 01:04:32.720
and people are listening
to it, and coming

01:04:32.720 --> 01:04:35.130
to my shows, and things,
it's absolutely wonderful.

01:04:35.130 --> 01:04:38.330
But it does truly feel
like a bonus to a process

01:04:38.330 --> 01:04:40.270
that I so thoroughly
enjoy that there'd

01:04:40.270 --> 01:04:41.960
be nothing that would
stop me doing it,

01:04:41.960 --> 01:04:44.720
if nobody was listening at all.

01:04:44.720 --> 01:04:51.050
So I think, to that end,
people say all sorts of things

01:04:51.050 --> 01:04:53.390
about people.

01:04:53.390 --> 01:04:56.240
And so you can be
bruised by something

01:04:56.240 --> 01:04:59.870
that somebody says
about you or to you,

01:04:59.870 --> 01:05:03.800
but only really if
you take their opinion

01:05:03.800 --> 01:05:07.080
as a method through which
you can validate yourself.

01:05:07.080 --> 01:05:13.370
And for me, I've always been of
the belief that I trust myself.

01:05:13.370 --> 01:05:15.380
I love my imagination so much.

01:05:15.380 --> 01:05:17.344
I just completely and
utterly believe in it.

01:05:17.344 --> 01:05:19.010
And it's not that I
always get it right.

01:05:19.010 --> 01:05:21.890
It's just that being in
there feels really good.

01:05:21.890 --> 01:05:27.050
And as a child, I just used to
sit in a room, just in heaven,

01:05:27.050 --> 01:05:29.540
with nothing else
other than just--

01:05:29.540 --> 01:05:35.795
because my mind was just
making all this stuff happen.

01:05:35.795 --> 01:05:37.670
I'm sure many of you
guys can relate to this.

01:05:37.670 --> 01:05:41.210
But one particular challenge is
that an overactive imagination

01:05:41.210 --> 01:05:43.670
in education is a
difficult thing to handle

01:05:43.670 --> 01:05:46.500
and a difficult thing to be
understood by those around you.

01:05:46.500 --> 01:05:49.546
And so for me, it was always,
Jacob, can you just do

01:05:49.546 --> 01:05:52.010
a bit less, just play simple.

01:05:52.010 --> 01:05:53.960
You've got lots of
good ideas, Jacob.

01:05:53.960 --> 01:05:56.410
Just one is enough.

01:05:56.410 --> 01:05:59.510
So I played a drum kit in
the concert band at school.

01:05:59.510 --> 01:06:01.050
And on the part it's like

01:06:01.050 --> 01:06:05.920
[BEAT BOXING]

01:06:05.920 --> 01:06:08.305
And it's like, OK, well

01:06:08.305 --> 01:06:11.205
[BEAT BOXING]

01:06:11.205 --> 01:06:12.830
And all these musicians
are just trying

01:06:12.830 --> 01:06:14.079
to play along with their part.

01:06:14.079 --> 01:06:15.920
And it's like,
everybody, stop, stop.

01:06:15.920 --> 01:06:20.670
OK, Jacob, less is
more, less is more.

01:06:20.670 --> 01:06:24.320
And it's that interesting
thing of less is more.

01:06:24.320 --> 01:06:27.220
Because less is only more
when you know what more is.

01:06:27.220 --> 01:06:30.260
And then, you can make
a conscious decision

01:06:30.260 --> 01:06:32.010
to step back from that.

01:06:32.010 --> 01:06:35.800
And for me, I think it's like
what I was saying earlier.

01:06:35.800 --> 01:06:38.330
It's about you have to
let yourself come out

01:06:38.330 --> 01:06:42.684
to be able to see yourself as
raw elements, raw materials.

01:06:42.684 --> 01:06:44.600
And then, you can make
some creative decisions

01:06:44.600 --> 01:06:45.308
about what to do.

01:06:45.308 --> 01:06:50.000
But if you're constantly
pushed back into the shell,

01:06:50.000 --> 01:06:53.962
not in terms of a very negative
way, like you're bad or wrong,

01:06:53.962 --> 01:06:56.420
but more just like there's too
much that we can't deal with

01:06:56.420 --> 01:07:00.850
right now, so just wait 'til--

01:07:00.850 --> 01:07:03.405
just right now, you just
got to play that groove.

01:07:03.405 --> 01:07:05.690
[BEAT BOXING]

01:07:05.690 --> 01:07:07.520
So I think for
me, as a teenager,

01:07:07.520 --> 01:07:10.139
I just had trouble relating
to the people around me that

01:07:10.139 --> 01:07:12.430
were telling me that things
were correct and incorrect.

01:07:12.430 --> 01:07:17.450
And I think I've learned since
that music, thank goodness,

01:07:17.450 --> 01:07:20.790
is not biology, for example.

01:07:20.790 --> 01:07:23.480
It's not a matter
of life and death.

01:07:23.480 --> 01:07:26.400
It's really just lovely.

01:07:26.400 --> 01:07:29.300
And the point of
music is to explore

01:07:29.300 --> 01:07:30.770
what it means to be expressive.

01:07:30.770 --> 01:07:34.144
And so there is not really
such a thing as this is correct

01:07:34.144 --> 01:07:35.060
and this is incorrect.

01:07:35.060 --> 01:07:36.320
And I've never paid
too much attention

01:07:36.320 --> 01:07:38.360
to this is the right
note to put in the chord

01:07:38.360 --> 01:07:40.160
and this is the wrong note.

01:07:40.160 --> 01:07:45.020
But I think, as I said, I've
trusted my intuitions enough

01:07:45.020 --> 01:07:47.700
to gather a sense of why
I feel this is strong

01:07:47.700 --> 01:07:50.780
and this is weak,
in terms of emotion.

01:07:50.780 --> 01:07:52.850
I'd maybe say that this voicing

01:07:52.850 --> 01:07:53.660
[PLAYS CHORD]

01:07:53.660 --> 01:07:54.800
Is not as strong as

01:07:54.800 --> 01:07:55.430
[PLAYS CHORD]

01:07:55.430 --> 01:07:56.450
This voicing.

01:07:56.450 --> 01:07:59.210
Because I don't necessarily
want to double the third,

01:07:59.210 --> 01:07:59.960
as Bach would say.

01:07:59.960 --> 01:08:01.376
And I later learned
that Bach said

01:08:01.376 --> 01:08:03.860
don't double the
third in principle.

01:08:03.860 --> 01:08:06.280
But I think it's--

01:08:06.280 --> 01:08:08.970
going back to the
original point,

01:08:08.970 --> 01:08:11.060
it's like, why do you do music?

01:08:11.060 --> 01:08:12.510
Why do you do anything?

01:08:12.510 --> 01:08:14.230
And for me, the
reason I do music

01:08:14.230 --> 01:08:15.980
is because I want to
chase those goosebump

01:08:15.980 --> 01:08:20.340
moments, the moments that just
make me go like, oh man, what

01:08:20.340 --> 01:08:21.140
does is that?

01:08:21.140 --> 01:08:22.115
What is that sound?

01:08:22.115 --> 01:08:23.240
And how do I understand it?

01:08:23.240 --> 01:08:25.939
How can I achieve that myself?

01:08:25.939 --> 01:08:28.220
And I looked around me as
a teenager and nobody else

01:08:28.220 --> 01:08:30.779
was thinking in those terms.

01:08:30.779 --> 01:08:33.050
So I just took upon myself
all the responsibilities

01:08:33.050 --> 01:08:37.910
of the band members and
gradually gathered things.

01:08:37.910 --> 01:08:42.429
But one problem I've had is
that my technique, my hands

01:08:42.429 --> 01:08:43.970
have never ever been
able to catch up

01:08:43.970 --> 01:08:46.714
with my head to the
point where, when

01:08:46.714 --> 01:08:48.380
I went to just college
as an 18-year-old

01:08:48.380 --> 01:08:50.660
for a couple of years, I
was the kid in the class

01:08:50.660 --> 01:08:53.060
with no chops but the ears.

01:08:53.060 --> 01:08:54.979
So I could hear everything
that was going on.

01:08:54.979 --> 01:08:56.353
I knew the notes,
and the chords,

01:08:56.353 --> 01:08:59.029
and why emotionally this
one note was just so tender,

01:08:59.029 --> 01:09:00.290
and stuff like that.

01:09:00.290 --> 01:09:03.439
But I couldn't just play
over Don Elliott 300 BPM.

01:09:03.439 --> 01:09:05.330
I couldn't do that.

01:09:05.330 --> 01:09:09.002
So it was hard to explain
why some things have value.

01:09:09.002 --> 01:09:11.210
It's hard to explain why
certain things are valuable,

01:09:11.210 --> 01:09:12.800
some underneath details.

01:09:12.800 --> 01:09:14.990
And I'm sure you guys,
and Jamshied, I'm sure,

01:09:14.990 --> 01:09:16.910
and absolutely Jean
can relate to this.

01:09:16.910 --> 01:09:19.580
Because it's to do with,
when you create something,

01:09:19.580 --> 01:09:22.250
it's tuning into all the
different levels of gestures,

01:09:22.250 --> 01:09:22.750
right?

01:09:22.750 --> 01:09:25.440
It's the general
gestures of it's this,

01:09:25.440 --> 01:09:28.100
and this is the beginning,
and this is the end.

01:09:28.100 --> 01:09:29.762
Or it's loud, and it's quiet.

01:09:29.762 --> 01:09:31.220
Or it's bright,
and then it's dark.

01:09:31.220 --> 01:09:34.010
But there are many
gestures in between

01:09:34.010 --> 01:09:36.275
that are to do with
it's bright, and it's

01:09:36.275 --> 01:09:38.149
heading towards brightness,
but I'm not going

01:09:38.149 --> 01:09:40.529
to make it bright until later.

01:09:40.529 --> 01:09:44.210
And suddenly, it's added
a dimension of intention

01:09:44.210 --> 01:09:47.630
that I just really
enjoyed as a teenager.

01:09:47.630 --> 01:09:50.540
So having the patience
to invest in those parts

01:09:50.540 --> 01:09:52.310
of your imagination
is a challenge.

01:09:52.310 --> 01:09:58.430
Because it's fairly, generally
misunderstood and/or dismissed.

01:09:58.430 --> 01:10:01.490
So I think there are always
times in a person's life

01:10:01.490 --> 01:10:04.119
where you have to think, well,
why am I doing this again?

01:10:04.119 --> 01:10:04.910
Why do I love this?

01:10:04.910 --> 01:10:07.370
And why is it important to me?

01:10:07.370 --> 01:10:09.950
And if, for example, my
left brain takes over,

01:10:09.950 --> 01:10:12.190
which it often does, I'm
sure some of you guys

01:10:12.190 --> 01:10:14.690
can relate to this thinking,
about thinking, about thinking,

01:10:14.690 --> 01:10:16.730
about thinking conundrum, right?

01:10:16.730 --> 01:10:18.050
And that's a problem.

01:10:18.050 --> 01:10:20.720
Because it's what you
described as paralysis

01:10:20.720 --> 01:10:24.260
from analysis type situations,
where you're just tied up.

01:10:24.260 --> 01:10:26.510
And you can't even see the
wood for the trees anymore.

01:10:26.510 --> 01:10:27.843
Because you've lost perspective.

01:10:27.843 --> 01:10:30.440
And all of us, as creative
people, can, I'm sure,

01:10:30.440 --> 01:10:32.840
relate to that thing
when you tune so deeply

01:10:32.840 --> 01:10:35.400
into a part of a process that
there's just no seeing it

01:10:35.400 --> 01:10:36.650
for what it is anymore.

01:10:36.650 --> 01:10:38.240
And that takes the courage
to step away, or get

01:10:38.240 --> 01:10:40.156
somebody else's opinion,
and not be defensive,

01:10:40.156 --> 01:10:42.760
and all sorts of things.

01:10:42.760 --> 01:10:45.440
But I think it's
coming back to a place

01:10:45.440 --> 01:10:48.260
that belongs to you, a place in
your mind that belongs to you,

01:10:48.260 --> 01:10:53.000
and where your values are
safe from others' judgment.

01:10:53.000 --> 01:10:56.920
And yeah, I could speak
for a long time about this.

01:10:56.920 --> 01:10:58.790
One other thing I
might quickly mention

01:10:58.790 --> 01:11:01.160
is that just touring was
exhausting at first for me.

01:11:01.160 --> 01:11:03.650
Because I'm certainly
an introvert.

01:11:03.650 --> 01:11:06.470
So concentrating on all
those little details,

01:11:06.470 --> 01:11:08.850
they kept me alive.

01:11:08.850 --> 01:11:13.040
And touring funnily enough, no
one really minds if its like--

01:11:13.040 --> 01:11:16.430
or-- it just doesn't make
much of a difference.

01:11:16.430 --> 01:11:19.940
Because everyone
just wants to go--

01:11:19.940 --> 01:11:22.970
So at first, it was like,
do you guys even care?

01:11:22.970 --> 01:11:25.850
And then, it's like, oh, now
I understand that actually,

01:11:25.850 --> 01:11:29.330
with communication, the
gestures have grown.

01:11:29.330 --> 01:11:32.640
The importance of putting the
foreground in the foreground

01:11:32.640 --> 01:11:33.310
has grown.

01:11:33.310 --> 01:11:34.850
And for me, when
I create things--

01:11:34.850 --> 01:11:36.920
I'm sure you guys would
relate a little bit

01:11:36.920 --> 01:11:38.836
to this as well-- it's
like this background is

01:11:38.836 --> 01:11:39.627
in the foreground.

01:11:39.627 --> 01:11:41.210
The underneaths are
in the foreground.

01:11:41.210 --> 01:11:42.820
Because they're
under the microscope.

01:11:42.820 --> 01:11:45.772
But when you perform,
that is the foreground.

01:11:45.772 --> 01:11:46.730
You are the foreground.

01:11:46.730 --> 01:11:49.490
And I had to realize that
it's the same energy that

01:11:49.490 --> 01:11:52.550
goes inwards that goes
outwards when one performs.

01:11:52.550 --> 01:11:55.550
But you just need to polarize
the direction of that energy.

01:11:55.550 --> 01:11:56.520
And then, you're fine.

01:11:56.520 --> 01:11:59.420
And I didn't need to build a
sort of Jacob the Champion who

01:11:59.420 --> 01:12:01.407
does touring and
excites people thing.

01:12:01.407 --> 01:12:03.240
It was just like I just
needed to be myself,

01:12:03.240 --> 01:12:04.810
but in a different direction.

01:12:04.810 --> 01:12:07.122
And so it's just like
falling off a log.

01:12:07.122 --> 01:12:09.080
You just think, well,
I'm going to be me today.

01:12:09.080 --> 01:12:11.371
And I don't really mind, even
if you like it very much.

01:12:11.371 --> 01:12:13.940
But it's just nice to see you,
sort of attitude to touring.

01:12:13.940 --> 01:12:15.231
I hope that helps a little bit.

01:12:15.231 --> 01:12:17.190
Yeah, is there--

01:12:17.190 --> 01:12:18.612
OK.

01:12:18.612 --> 01:12:22.084
[APPLAUSE]

01:12:29.524 --> 01:12:32.560
I was going to say, is there
a place where-- because I know

01:12:32.560 --> 01:12:33.730
you're a very busy person.

01:12:33.730 --> 01:12:37.690
But if you could share a little
bit more of your insights

01:12:37.690 --> 01:12:39.827
with me, when you find the time.

01:12:39.827 --> 01:12:40.660
We could figure out.

01:12:40.660 --> 01:12:41.970
Yeah, I'd very much enjoy that.

01:12:41.970 --> 01:12:42.910
OK, great.

01:12:42.910 --> 01:12:45.440
It's a date.

01:12:45.440 --> 01:12:47.116
Just give me something.

01:12:47.116 --> 01:12:49.060
Perfect, cool.

01:12:54.030 --> 01:13:00.340
So we are certainly so
grateful to have Jacob

01:13:00.340 --> 01:13:01.720
as a part of our community.

01:13:01.720 --> 01:13:04.240
We hope he continues to
be part of our community.

01:13:04.240 --> 01:13:09.940
And he's a world citizen
these days, along with Ben.

01:13:09.940 --> 01:13:12.700
And we thought it'd
be great to end

01:13:12.700 --> 01:13:14.020
with Jacob performing for us.

01:13:14.020 --> 01:13:15.223
Jacob Collier.

01:13:15.223 --> 01:13:18.604
[APPLAUSE]

01:13:26.350 --> 01:13:28.570
I just need to apply
some tape to my face,

01:13:28.570 --> 01:13:30.159
with the help of Mr. Bloomberg.

01:13:38.472 --> 01:13:41.895
[APPLAUSE]

01:13:49.230 --> 01:13:51.600
So meet the harmonizer.

01:13:51.600 --> 01:13:52.530
He's a lovely beastie.

01:14:06.418 --> 01:14:10.386
